if you've been thinking about getting a  portable SSD for your computer this  video is for you we're gonna compare  four of the top portable SSDs to see  which one is the better Buy in terms of  various categories like design size  portability performance and more and  we're not only going to run the standard  performance benchmarks but we're also  gonna transfer massive files over to  these drives to heat them up and see  which drive performs the best under  stress and let me tell you there  definitely were a few surprises you  don't want to miss these aren't any  regular SSDs they're the fastest ones  out there and that's because they all  use Thunderbolt 3 which allows for  transfer speeds that are over five times  as fast as regular USB C SSDs and since  every single new Mac in most new Windows  laptops and many desktop computers are  starting to come with Thunderbolt 3  ports these SSDs are the future so let's  compare everything about these for  portable SSDs to see which one you  should buy in 2020 first off we've got  Samsung's super clean almost Tesla like  x5 SSD then we've got the tried-and-true  LaCie rugged SSD pro we've also got the  G technology G Drive mobile pro by  Western Digital which is also very  reliable and finally the envoi Pro X or  X from the well known Mac centric brand  o WC now before we get into this  comparison I want to mention that each  of these drives has one terabyte of  storage which I would say is the best  choice for the price let's start off our  comparison with portability the o WC is  by far the bulkiest drive out of the  bunch followed by the G Drive and then  the Samsung x5 with the LaCie drive  being the most portable by far in terms  of design I think the x5 takes the cake  with the really unique shape painted  finish in accents on the side and bottom  it's the only one that really screams  premium the LaCie is the runner up with  a really simple and clean black design  and while I don't really like the extra  blue accents on the G Drive it still  looks better than the O WCS brick like  design now in terms of durability the  LaCie wins by far  covered with a rubber bumper all around  so it won't get scratched up like some  of the others it also includes pressure  shock dust and even ip67 water  resistance which none of the others have  and it also has three meter drop  protection the Samsung x5 actually  includes a metal protection frame on the  inside to protect the SSD so it's rated  to resist a drop from around six feet  but the plastic frame could still crack  the g-drive is actually rated with an  impressive 10 feet of drop protection  and while the in void Pro X is built out  of aluminum and comes with the rubber  bumper it doesn't have any official drop  ratings but it should still be pretty  durable now let's compare the included  Thunderbolt three cables this time both  the Samsung x5 and the G Drive came with  high quality cables that are around a  foot and a half long the LaCie SSDs  cable was actually less than half as  long which may be nice for portability  but it's restricting if you want to use  the cable for anything else and the o WC  cable is short and permanently attached  to the SSD itself which you obviously  can't lose but it could lead to a  nightmare if the cable gets damaged in  any way more on this in just a bit in  terms of the warranties the LaCie beats  the rest of these out by far what the  five-year warranty a five-year data  recovery service plan and a one-month  subscription to all of the Adobe  Creative Cloud apps the G Drive also  comes with a five-year warranty but the  OWCA and Samsung drives only come with a  three-year warranty which is still  pretty decent but it doesn't match up to  the others now let's get into  performance before running our speed  tests we reformatted all the drives 2x  fat which is the most universal large  file format that works on both Mac OS  and Windows 10 for our first test we use  black magics disk speed test to see the  maximum speed these drafts can run at  using the 5 gigabyte file option the  Samsung x5 was the fastest by about a  hundred megabytes per second which is  pretty impressive but we didn't see  speeds nearing the advertised 2800  megabytes per second in terms of the  write speeds the G Drive was actually  the fastest with write speeds of up to  two thousand two hundred and eighty  megabytes per second so between those  two benchmarks it seems like it's a draw  between the Samsung and the G Drive now  let's get into the stress test where we  transferred a 172 gigabyte file to and  from the drives in our Mac Pro which  runs fast enough to not limit the drives  we noticed that when transferring the  file to each Drive the right speed  slowed down after a certain amount of  data was transferred after around 48  gigabytes the Samsung x5 slowed down to  around 1200 megabytes per second for the  G Drive it only took 20 gigabytes of  data to slow the drive down to fourteen  hundred megabytes per second faster than  the Samsung now for the LaCie  it slowed down to a much lower 1000  megabytes per second after 30 gigabytes  of data was transferred and finally the  owz drive was actually the worst slowing  down to 950 megabytes per second after  30 gigs of data now if you don't  understand how SLC cache works let me  explain it to you on each of these  drives the storage is made up of  multiple storage chips now in most  drives there are some very fast chips  and then the rest of them are slower in  the case of the LaCie drive it has 30  gigs of fast chips that can write at a  very fast a thousand nine hundred and  thirty megabytes per second but anything  beyond that up to the rest of the 970  gigs slows down to around a thousand  megabytes per second that means if  you're transferring a file from your  computer to the drive that's over 30  gigabytes large it'll slow down to the  slower speeds after 30 gigs have been  transferred so we actually tested how  long it took to transfer our 172 gig  file to each of the drives and the G  Drive was the fastest because even  though it had the least amount of cache  the speed of the slower chips was the  fastest at 1,400 megabytes per second  the Samsung x5 was in second place  because I had the most SLC cache in the  slower chips were almost as fast as the  chips on the G Drive and out of all of  them the odbc drive was the slowest now  let's move on to a read speed  overheating test we transferred about  500 gigabytes of data from each drive to  our Mac Pro without give  them a chance to cool down to test the  cooling performance and all but one  drive performed perfectly fine not  slowing down throughout the entire 500  gigs of data that one drive actually  kept ejecting itself over and over again  so we couldn't even transfer over 200  gigs of data non-stop and that drive was  the ODA VC envoi Pro we tried  reformatting it multiple times and  nothing would help we're not sure if we  had a bad drive or not but we didn't  experience any issues with the other  drives to make it even worse the cable  is attached permanently so we couldn't  even test out a different cable to see  if that was the issue so in terms of  performance the G Drive is the winner  with the Samsung being the runner-up and  the ODBC being the worst because of the  ejection issues now we actually dug deep  into each drive to figure out which SSD  they're packing and we were able to find  the tbw stats for each drive which is  basically the amount of data you can  write on the drive before it starts to  fail into our surprise the LaCie Drive  was rated for a much higher amount of  data written compared to the other  drives blowing them away in terms of  reliability with the ODBC drive being  the worst in one last thing the LaCie  drive is actually the only drive out of  the bunch that also works with USB C  allowing you to transfer data between  your Mac and other Windows devices that  don't have Thunderbolt 3 now for our  final category price now I want to  mention that prices fluctuate all the  time so make sure to check the links  down in the description below  the Samsung x5 is currently available  for 450 dollars on bnh and actually 350  dollars on Amazon but we do expect a  price to go down on bnh to match Amazon  soon the G Drive is currently 380  dollars the lacy is 400 dollars and the  owz Drive is a much lower two hundred  and eighty dollars so with all of that  comparison testing finished let's finish  with their purchasing recommendations  now although the owz drive is the  cheapest we would not recommend it  because the bulky size and the  permanently attached cable which can get  damaged and render the drive useless now  if you're looking for the most  rugged and reliable drive without caring  too much about having the absolute best  performance we would go with the lazy  because of its excellent portability  rugged design ip67 water resistance  backwards compatibility with USBC  excellent reliability and longer 5-year  warranties and data rescue services now  if you're not planning on transferring  very large files we would actually  recommend the samsung x5 because of its  sleek design long Thunderbolt 3 cable  and massive 48 gigs of SOC cache that  will help it be the fastest drive for  transferring a lot of small files  now while the G Drive doesn't have the  best looking or compact design it gets  10 feet of drop protection a nice 5 year  warranty along cable and by far the  fastest performance out of all of these  drives at $380 is definitely not the  cheapest out of the bunch but it  excelled in a lot of the categories and  blew the competition away in terms of  performance but once again if you're all  about reliability and you want the  freedom to use your drive with not only  Thunderbolt 3 but also USB C then the  LaCie drive easily beats out the rest  and without a doubt that's definitely  the drive that we would purchase  ourselves now we're actually going to be  building our own custom DIY portable SSD  using a Thunderbolt 3 nvme SSD enclosure  in a PCI Express 4.0 m2 SSD to see if we  can beat the speeds of these drives at a  much lower price so if you're interested  in seeing that click that circle above  to subscribe and check out those two  videos right over there thanks for  watching and we'll see you guys in the  next video  [Music]

